faqs:
  - question: "What is a reason in Appwrite Activity?"
    answer: "A reason is one line of text that says why an API request was made, such as \"Remove the test users created while debugging sign-up\". The client sends it in the `X-Appwrite-Reason` header. Appwrite stores it on the request's audit log entry and shows it in the **Reason** column of your project's **Activity** tab and in the event's details."
  - question: "How do I see why an AI agent changed my Appwrite project?"
    answer: "Connect the agent through the [Appwrite MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers). The server asks the agent for a reason with every change it makes and sends it with the request. Open your project's **Activity** tab to see each change, marked **Via MCP**, with the reason the agent gave."
  - question: "How do I add a reason to Appwrite CLI commands?"
    answer: "Pass `--reason \"...\"` to any command, or set the `APPWRITE_REASON` environment variable for a whole shell session or CI job. The flag takes precedence over the variable. Every request the command makes carries the reason. See [Reason](/docs/tooling/command-line/commands#reason)."
  - question: "Can I send a reason from my own code?"
    answer: "Yes. Send the `X-Appwrite-Reason` header with any API request, percent-encoded with `encodeURIComponent` or your language's equivalent. With a server SDK, add it to the client with `addHeader`. See [Record a reason](/docs/advanced/security/audit-logs#reason)."
  - question: "What makes a reason valid?"
    answer: "A reason is one line of valid UTF-8 text of up to 256 characters. Line breaks, tabs, other control characters, and Unicode bidirectional override characters aren't allowed. A request with an invalid reason fails with `general_argument_invalid` before it runs, so nothing changes."
  - question: "Is a reason required?"
    answer: "Not for your own requests or CLI commands; events without one show `-` in the **Reason** column. The Appwrite MCP server is the exception: it requires a reason for every write its tools make, and refuses a write without one before it reaches your project."
---

AI agents and scripts now make a large share of the changes in many Appwrite projects. An agent cleans up test data, a CI job pushes new functions, a teammate runs a one-off migration from their terminal. The **Activity** tab already recorded who made each change and what it touched, but not the one thing a reviewer most wants to know: why it happened.

Today, we are announcing **reasons in Activity**. Every API request can now carry a short note about why it was made, and Appwrite shows that note next to the change it explains. The Appwrite MCP server records a reason for every change an AI agent makes, the Appwrite CLI takes one with a single flag, and any other client can send one with a header.

# What's new

- **A Reason column in Activity.** Each event in your project's **Activity** tab shows the reason it was made, and the event's details show the full text.
- **Reasons for every agent change.** The [Appwrite MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers#reasons) asks the AI agent for a one-line reason with each write and refuses a write without one.
- **Reasons from the CLI.** Any [Appwrite CLI](/docs/tooling/command-line/commands#reason) command takes a reason, from a flag or from an environment variable that covers a whole CI job.
- **Reasons from any client.** Your own scripts and services can send a reason with any API request, so changes made by your own code are just as easy to trace.

# Review what an AI agent changed

{% only_dark %}
![Project Activity tab with reasons](/images/blog/announcing-activity-reasons/activity-dark.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![Project Activity tab with reasons](/images/blog/announcing-activity-reasons/activity.avif)
{% /only_light %}

When an agent works on your project through the [Appwrite MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers), every write it makes now comes with a reason. The server's write tool takes a required `reason` alongside the request, so the agent has to say what it's doing and why before it changes anything. A missing, multi-line, or overlong reason is refused before any request reaches your project, with an error that tells the agent how to fix it.

The reason lands in **Activity** next to the change, on an event already marked **Via MCP**. An agent session that cleaned up users, changed a table, and redeployed a function reads as a list of decisions, each with the agent's own explanation, such as "Remove the test users created while debugging sign-up". Reads don't carry a reason, so the log stays focused on what changed.

This works with the hosted MCP server and with the server running locally, in any MCP client.

# Record reasons from the CLI and CI

Add `--reason` to any Appwrite CLI command, and every request the command makes carries it:

```sh
appwrite push functions --reason "Release 2.4: move checkout to the storefront site"
```

In CI, set the `APPWRITE_REASON` environment variable once for the whole job instead. Tie it to the commit and branch, and every change a deploy makes in your project points back to the run that made it:

```yaml
env:
  APPWRITE_REASON: "Deploy ${{ github.sha }} from ${{ github.ref_name }}"
```

A `--reason` flag takes precedence over the variable, and those events show up in **Activity** marked **Via CLI**.

# Send a reason from your own code

Any client can send a reason in the `X-Appwrite-Reason` header. With a server SDK, add the header to the client, and every request that client makes carries it:

```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>')
    .addHeader('X-Appwrite-Reason', encodeURIComponent('Archive orders older than two years'));
```

Use a separate client for each reason, so a reason meant for one job doesn't follow unrelated requests. A reason is one line of up to 256 characters, in any language. Appwrite percent-decodes the header, so encode the value with `encodeURIComponent` or your language's equivalent. A request with an invalid reason, such as one with a line break or a hidden control character, fails before it runs and changes nothing.

# What makes a good reason

A good reason answers the question a teammate would ask while scrolling the log a week later. "Update rows" repeats what the event already says. "Fix prices imported with the wrong currency from the October CSV" tells the reviewer whether the change was intended and where to look if it wasn't.

For agents, the MCP server's instructions already ask the model for a reason with every write. For scripts and CI, include whatever links the change back to its source: a commit, a ticket, a pipeline run, or the name of the job.

# Know why every change happened

As more of the work in a project is done by agents and automation, the audit log is where a team builds trust in that work. Reasons turn the log from a record of what happened into a record of what was intended, so reviewing an agent's session or tracking down an unexpected change takes a glance instead of guesswork. We'd love to hear how your team uses them, so share your feedback with us on [Discord](https://appwrite.io/discord).

